  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/e00248d1dd5cb96a5e0099acf5da41913a805dbc388715f0b542aa821a95efa5.png)\n\nThe scatterplot above shows the relationship between the number of hours $x$ a group of people spend watching TV each night and the time $y$ they sleep each night. The equation of the trend line is $y=10-1.25x.$ \n\nAssuming this trend continues, estimate the time a person sleeps per night if they watch TV for $6$ hours.",
  "worked_solution": "To predict the number of hours a person sleeps after watching TV for $6$ hours, we need to substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the trend line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =10-1.25x \\\\  & =10-1.25(6) \\\\  & =10-7.5 \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, according to this model, a person watching TV for $6$ hours sleeps for approximately $2.5$ hours.\n\nNote: The point where $x=6$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable.",
